  "full_text": "Hello and welcome back to JD science. Today we are exploring plant tissues and organs. A leaf has several tissues. The epidermis covers the leaf. The palisade mesophyll is packed with chloroplasts for photosynthesis. The spongy mesophyll has air spaces for gas exchange. Xylem and phloem form the veins. Guard cells control the stomata which control gas exchange and water loss. Water evaporates through the stomata which pulls water up the xylem. This is transpiration. Transpiration is faster in warm, dry, windy conditions and in bright light. Xylem carries water and mineral ions upwards. Phloem carries sugars up and down the plant. Transpiration is the loss of water vapour from leaves, mainly through stomata. Water evaporates from mesophyll cell surfaces and diffuses into the air. This creates tension that helps draw a continuous column of water up the xylem. Phloem transports dissolved sugars from sources such as photosynthesising leaves to sinks such as roots and growing fruits. The direction depends on the location of sources and sinks. As you revise this topic, try to explain the key process in your own words. Focus on what happens, why it happens and how the structures involved help the process work. Now, pause the video and try this exam style question. Name the plant tissue that carries sugars and describe the direction it carries them. Here is a model answer. Phloem, it carries sugars up and down the plant. If this explanation has helped, please subscribe to JD Science for more lessons. You can find further learning resources and practice questions at jdscience.co.uk. Thank you for watching.",
